# boundaries.denylist — what the host may not carry. Read by scripts/check-boundaries.sh:
# one extended regex per line, joined with '|', matched case-sensitively over every
# tracked text file. Governed by the handbook's concepts/boundaries.md (host row: "any
# domain noun: document, row, model, agent, deal") and AGENTS.md §"Boundaries".
#
# This gate is ADDITIVE to the gates the repo already runs. Customer, partner and
# consumer names are enforced by module/tools/naming-gate.mjs, by digest, and this file
# deliberately does not spell them (AGENTS.md §"Naming and confidentiality" binds this
# file too). Generic technical nouns the host legitimately uses (document, row, model,
# agent) are a review rule, not a grep rule.

# A product noun that belongs to a business line, never to the host: the type/proper-noun
# form and the identifier forms. The lowercase English verb ("deal with", the MIT licence's
# "deal in the Software") is not matched on purpose.
\bDeals?\b
\bdeals?[-_](id|type|intelligence|book|room|flow)s?\b
\bdeal[-_ ]intelligence\b

# Solution names contributed on top of the host: a solution is installed at runtime and
# the host has no build-time knowledge of it, so its name appears nowhere here — not in
# fixtures, not in a proxy path, not in a catalogue's "what the current solutions need".
# A "/wiki/" path segment is excluded because it is how GitHub and other hosts address a
# project wiki (github.com/<org>/<repo>/wiki/<page>), which is not a solution name; the
# solution-shaped paths are then matched explicitly. The second solution's slug is matched
# only in its all-lowercase form: `lastLogin`/`LastLogin` is the host's own identity
# vocabulary (a user's last login timestamp), not the solution.
(^|[^/])\b[Ww]iki\b
\bsolutions/[Ww]iki\b
/s/[Ww]iki\b
\blastlogin\b

# Another module's name and internals: the host hands work to "the consuming module"
# through its ports and never knows which one. (A line that says who consumes the host
# is exempted by the script — the one place the handbook allows naming a consumer.)
\b[Dd]ocument[-_ ][Ss]tores?\b
